.TH bcrun 1 "FUZIX System Utilities" "FUZIX"
.SH NAME
bcrun, bcdump \- run and disassemble a compiled program
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bcrun
.I file.bc
[\fIargument\fR ...]
.br
.B bcdump
.I file.bc
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bcrun
runs what
.BR cc (1)
produces.  You rarely name it: a
.B .bc
file is executable in its own right and finds
.B bcrun
by itself, so
.B ./prog.bc
is the usual way.
.PP
.B bcdump
disassembles a
.B .bc
file, which is how to see what the compiler made of something \- in
particular, which functions became native ARM and which stayed as
bytecode.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BCRUN_BYTECODE
Set to 1 to run everything as bytecode, ignoring the native spans.  This
is for comparing the two, and makes a hot loop two to three times
slower.
.SH RECURSION
.B bcrun
checks the stack when a bytecode function is entered and stops the
program cleanly if it has run out.  A function the compiler turned into
native ARM has a three-instruction prologue with no check in it \- and a
small recursive function is exactly the kind that gets translated.  A
missing terminating condition on one of those is a reset of the machine,
not an error message.  Honest depth is about 237 frames.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR cc (1),
.BR mmbc (1)
